        'synopsis' => 'A Roman mother in a plain white robe answers her rich friend\'s glittering casket with the only jewels she owns, her two sons.',
        'text' => "It was a bright morning in Rome many hundred years ago. In a vine-covered summer house in a beautiful garden two boys stood watching their mother and her friend walk among the flowers.\n\n\"Did you ever see so handsome a lady as our mother's friend?\" asked the younger boy. \"She looks like a queen.\"\n\n\"She is not so beautiful as our mother,\" answered the elder. \"Her dress is fine indeed, but her face is not noble and kind. It is our mother who is like a queen.\"\n\nSoon Cornelia came down the walk to speak with them. She wore a plain white robe, without ring or chain upon her hands and neck, and her only crown was made of long braids of soft brown hair coiled about her head. A tender smile lit up her noble face.\n\n\"You are to dine with us in the garden today,\" she told the boys. \"Then our friend will show us that wonderful casket of jewels of which you have heard so much.\"\n\nWhen the simple meal was over, a servant brought out the casket. How its treasures dazzled the eyes of the wondering boys! There were ropes of pearls white as milk, heaps of rubies red as glowing coals, sapphires blue as the summer sky, and diamonds that flashed like sunlight.\n\n\"If only our mother could have such beautiful things,\" whispered the younger boy.\n\nAt last the casket was closed and carried carefully away. \"Is it true, Cornelia, that you have no jewels?\" asked her friend. \"Is it true that you are poor?\"\n\n\"No, I am not poor,\" answered Cornelia, drawing her two sons to her side. \"For here are my jewels, and they are worth more than all your gems.\"\n\nThe boys never forgot their mother's pride and love. In after years they became great men in Rome, and the world still likes to hear the story of Cornelia's jewels.",
