        'text' => "A cruel battle was being fought. The ground was covered with dead and dying men, the air was hot and stifling, and the sun shone down without pity on the wounded soldiers lying in the blood and dust.\n\nAmong them was a nobleman whom everybody loved for his gentleness and kindness. Yet now he was no better off than the poorest man in the field. He had been wounded, he knew that he would die, and he suffered greatly from pain and thirst.\n\nWhen the fighting paused, his friends hurried to his aid. A soldier came running with a cup in his hand.\n\n\"Here, Sir Philip,\" he said. \"I have brought you some clear cool water from the brook.\"\n\nThe cup was placed to Sir Philip Sidney's lips, and how thankfully he looked at the man who had brought it! Then his eyes met those of a dying soldier who was lying on the ground close by. The wistful look in the poor man's face spoke plainer than words.\n\n\"Give the water to that man,\" said Sidney quickly, pushing the cup toward him. \"Here, my comrade, take this. Thy need is greater than mine.\"\n\nWhat a brave, noble man he was! His name will never be forgotten, for it was the name of a Christian gentleman who always held the good of others in mind.\n\nAll the land wept when it was heard that he was dead. On the day they carried him to the grave, every eye was filled with tears. Rich and poor alike mourned the death of the kindest, gentlest man that they had ever known.",
