        'synopsis' => 'A soap king\'s son swears money cannot buy time with the girl he loves, until a suspiciously perfect traffic jam proves his father right.',
        'text' => "Old Anthony Rockwall, retired maker of Eureka Soap, looked out from his Fifth Avenue mansion and grinned at the aristocratic clubman next door, who always wrinkled his nose at the soap palace beside his own fine house.\n\nHe called his son Richard into the library and questioned him about the price of his soap and his suits. \"You're a gentleman,\" the old man declared. \"They say it takes three generations to make one. They're wrong. Money will do it as slick as soap grease.\"\n\n\"There are some things money can't accomplish,\" said Richard gloomily.\n\n\"Now don't say that,\" cried Anthony, shocked. \"I bet my money on money every time. Tell me something it won't buy.\"\n\nRichard confessed his trouble. He loved Miss Lantry, but every hour of her time was arranged days in advance, and she was sailing for Europe in two days. His only chance was to meet her train at Grand Central and drive her to the theater where her mother would wait, six or eight minutes alone in a cab and not one second more.\n\n\"You say money won't buy time,\" chuckled his father. \"I've seen Father Time get pretty bad stone bruises when he walked through the gold diggings.\"\n\nThat evening gentle Aunt Ellen gave Richard a quaint old gold ring that had belonged to his mother. Good luck in love, she said, it always brought.\n\nAt the station he captured Miss Lantry, and the cab whirled away toward Broadway. At Thirty-fourth Street Richard suddenly stopped the driver, climbed out, and searched the pavement for the ring he claimed to have dropped.\n\nWithin that single minute a crosstown car, a furniture van, and what seemed like the whole traffic of Manhattan converged on the crossing and locked into a hopeless tangle. For two hours no wheel could turn.\n\n\"It looks as if we are stuck,\" said Richard, climbing back inside. \"It was all my fault. If I had not dropped that ring, we would never have been delayed.\"\n\n\"Let me see the ring,\" said Miss Lantry. \"Now that it can't be helped, I don't mind at all. I think theaters are stupid anyway.\"\n\nBefore the street was cleared she had promised to marry him.\n\nAt eleven o'clock Aunt Ellen drifted into the library like a silver-haired angel left on earth by mistake. \"They're engaged, Anthony,\" she whispered, and begged him never again to boast of money, since a little emblem of true love had worked this miracle.\n\nAnthony only wished to go on reading about his pirate. The next day a man named Kelly presented his account, and Anthony paid every dollar without blinking, the wages of wagons, trucks, motormen, and a pair of policemen who could knot up Manhattan for exactly two hours.\n\nAs Kelly reached the door, Anthony called after him. \"Did you notice, anywhere in that tie-up, a kind of fat boy without any clothes on, shooting arrows around with a bow?\"\n\n\"Why no, sir,\" said puzzled Kelly. \"If he looked like that, maybe the cops pinched him before I got there.\"\n\n\"I thought as much,\" chuckled Anthony.",
