        'synopsis' => 'A timid orchid collector raises a blood-drinking bloom from a dead explorer\'s last find and survives only thanks to his frightened housekeeper.',
        'text' => "Buying orchids always has a speculative flavor about it. You hold a brown shriveled lump of tissue, and for the rest you must trust your judgment or your luck.\n\nWinter-Wedderburn was a shy, lonely, rather ineffective man with just enough income to keep off the spur of necessity and one ambitious little hothouse. He grew orchids because nothing else of the slightest interest ever happened to him, and nothing ever had.\n\n\"I have a fancy,\" he told his housekeeper one morning, \"that something is going to happen to me today.\"\n\n\"Don't say that!\" cried his cousin, for to her such words could mean only one thing. But he merely meant that Peters' sale of plants from the Andamans was on, and he intended to be there.\n\nHe returned in mild excitement and spread his purchases on the tablecloth. Most were ordinary, but one shriveled rhizome had never been identified at all. It might prove a new species, or even a new genus, and it was the very last orchid that poor young Batten ever collected. Batten had been found lying dead in a mangrove swamp with this crushed thing beneath his body, and every drop of blood, they said, had been taken from him by the jungle leeches.\n\n\"It looks like a spider shamming dead,\" said the housekeeper, who disliked it from the start.\n\nFor days Wedderburn fussed happily among charcoal, moss, and teak chunks in his steamy glass house. Several plants died under his care, but the strange one woke. A bud appeared, then broad glossy leaves splashed with red, and little white rootlets pushing out into the air. They looked to the housekeeper like fingers trying to get at you, and after one glance she refused ever to enter the hothouse again, because those rootlets followed her into her dreams and grew after her like reaching tentacles.\n\nWedderburn told her instead about Darwin and the fertilization of orchids, and how certain flowers seemed designed for no insect known to science. Very likely, he added, his own orchid might be extraordinary in exactly that way.\n\nAt last the great thing happened. Entering the glass house, he caught a new odor, rich and intensely sweet, and found three great splashes of blossom on the trailing green spikes, white streaked with golden orange, their coiled lips mingling blue purple with gold. He saw at once that the genus was altogether new. Then the perfume and the steamy heat made the floor dance, and he pitched forward senseless at the plant's foot.\n\nAt half past four his cousin came to call him to tea and found him lying face upward beneath the strange orchid. Its gray ropelike rootlets no longer swayed freely but stretched tight against his chin, neck, and hands, and a thread of blood trickled from under one upon his cheek.\n\nShe snapped two of the suckers, and their sap ran red. Fighting the overpowering scent, she smashed the greenhouse windows with a flower pot for air, dragged her master's body clear, and tore the clinging rootlets away one by one. He was white and bleeding from a dozen circular patches, but he lived.\n\nBy morning the strange orchid lay black and putrescent on the bricks, and every tender plant in the collection stood shriveled and prostrate beside it. Upstairs, however, Wedderburn sat bright and garrulous, glowing in the glory of the strangest thing that had ever happened to him.",
