        'synopsis' => 'A mesmerist freezes a dying friend at the very door of death and holds him there for seven months, until the day he dares to wake him.',
        'text' => "Of course I shall not pretend it any matter for wonder that the extraordinary case of Monsieur Valdemar has excited so much discussion. Garbled and exaggerated accounts made their way into society and became the source of many misrepresentations and much disbelief. It is now necessary that I give the facts.\n\nFor three years my attention had been drawn to mesmerism, and it occurred to me one day that a remarkable omission ran through every experiment yet made. No person had ever been mesmerized at the very point of death. Could the magnetic influence still reach a dying man? Might the encroachments of death itself be arrested, and for how long?\n\nThe subject who offered himself was my friend Ernest Valdemar, well known as a compiler and translator, a spare man with white whiskers and black hair, and marked by a nervous temperament suited to such work. For months his physicians had pronounced him dying of consumption, and he spoke calmly of his approaching end as of a thing neither to be avoided nor regretted. When I laid my purpose before him, his interest blazed up, and we arranged that he would send for me about a day before the hour his doctors had fixed.\n\nTheir calculation proved exact. His note reached me on a Saturday evening. You may as well come now, it said, for I cannot hold out beyond tomorrow midnight.\n\nI found him terribly altered, propped on pillows and penciling memoranda, the cheekbones breaking through the skin, his pulse barely perceptible. Two physicians attended and could give little hope, fixing his death about midnight on Sunday. A medical student arrived at eight to record whatever should occur, and although the dying man urged me to begin at once, I waited for that witness before making the first pass.\n\nHe whispered that he feared I had deferred it too long. The lateral strokes subdued him quickly, yet no deeper effect followed until nearly eleven o'clock, when at last his glazing eye took on the unmistakable expression of sleep-waking. With further passes I closed his lids and stiffened his limbs where they lay, and by midnight both doctors pronounced him in a perfect mesmeric trance.\n\nAt three in the morning I found him cold as marble, without pulse or perceptible breath, yet surely not looking like a dead man. His right arm followed my hand as it never had in life, and after I had repeated my question three times, a shiver passed over him and a whisper crept out between the sluggish lips. Yes, asleep now. Do not wake me. Let me die so.\n\nThe physicians agreed that death stood arrested, but that waking him would insure instant dissolution. Before sunrise he answered again that he still slept and was dying.\n\nThen came the change that none of us was fit to see. As I spoke once more, his eyes rolled slowly open, his skin turned the white of paper, the hectic spots upon his cheeks went out like candles snuffed by a breath, and his jaw fell with an audible jerk, disclosing a swollen and blackened tongue. Everyone present shrank back from the bed.\n\nConcluding him dead, we were surrendering him to the nurses when a strong vibration shook the tongue. From the distended jaws issued a voice which it would be madness to describe, harsh, broken, and hollow, seeming to reach us from a vast distance or from some deep cavern within the earth, and clinging to the sense like some gelatinous stuff. Yet every syllable was distinct, for he was answering my old question. Yes, no, I have been sleeping, and now, now, I am dead.\n\nThe student swooned, the nurses fled and could not be persuaded to return, and we spent an hour in silence reviving the young man. Afterward the mirror showed no breath upon its glass, the lancet could draw no blood, and only when I myself spoke did the tongue quiver in reply. Other nurses were procured, and for nearly seven months we called daily and found the sleep-waker lying exactly as he lay that night.\n\nOnly last Friday did we resolve to attempt the experiment of waking him. At my passes a yellowish ichor flowed from beneath his lids, and when I asked what were his feelings and wishes, the same hideous voice broke forth. For God's sake, quick, put me to sleep, or quick, waken me. I say to you that I am dead.\n\nDead, dead, burst rather from the tongue than from the lips while I struggled desperately to recall the trance. And within the space of a single minute his whole frame shrunk, crumbled, and absolutely rotted away beneath my hands. Upon the bed, before the whole company, there lay a nearly liquid mass of loathsome and detestable putrescence.",
