    [
        'slug' => 'scifi-diamond-lens',
        'gutenberg_id' => 50332,
        'title' => 'The Diamond Lens',
        'author' => 'Fitz-James O\'Brien',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science Fiction'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'hard',
        'cover' => 'covers/scifi-diamond-lens.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A young scientist kills for a spirit-promised diamond lens and the microscopic paradise it reveals costs him his reason and the tiny love he finds inside it.',
        'text' => "From my tenth year every bent of my mind ran toward the microscope. A cousin drilled a hole in a copper disk, held in it a drop of pure water, and let me gaze into another universe. Where others saw rain rolling down a windowpane, I saw beings convulsing their minute sphere with struggles as fierce as those of men, and in the mildew scraped from jam pots I found enchanted gardens hung with strange fruits of green and silver and gold. The rod of my life was bent at that moment. I destined myself to be a microscopist.\n\nTo escape the counting house my family intended for me, I went to New York pretending to study medicine, equipped myself with the finest instruments money could buy, and within a year became an accomplished observer. Yet at every step the imperfections of my lenses stopped me, and after two more years of grinding glass, gems, and flints I stood precisely where I began, nearly dead with despair. I dreamed of a perfect simple lens whose magnifying power should be limited only by the resolvability of the object.\n\nThen Jules Simon, a mysterious Frenchman who lodged above me and peddled rare single objects, happened to mention Madame Vulpes, a spirit medium whose answers to secretly written questions had frightened him with their truth. I visited her, concealed the name Leeuwenhoek on a slip beneath the table, and watched her trembling hand produce the words, I am here. Question me. The spirit of the father of microscopists revealed that a diamond of one hundred and forty carats, submitted long to electromagnetic currents, would experience a rearrangement of its atoms, and from that stone I could form the universal lens, pierced through its axis so that the image would form within the bore.\n\nThat very night I let slip to Simon my wish for such a stone, and the little man flew upon a Malay dagger, shrieking that I should never have his treasure and that he would die first. I soothed him, produced a case of famous Burgundy, and poured until his weak head swam. He then confessed that a vast rose diamond named the Eye of Morning lay hidden against his breast, taken while he superintended slaves washing diamonds in Brazil, too notorious a gem for him to dare sell.\n\nI sat opposite his nodding head and revolved the matter calmly. What was the life of such a creature compared with the interests of science? Criminals were handed to surgeons every day for experimentation. I drugged his glass, laid him on the bed, and drove his own dagger up to the hilt, arranging every circumstance as self-murder. His fingers even clasped the handle in a last spasm, as though to assist my plan. I burned papers on the hearth, rinsed one wineglass and loaded the other with laudanum to explain what an examination might find, carried the bottles away, and locked his door behind me with a slender vise turned through the keyhole. The coroner's jury needed no help reaching the usual verdict, since half the city had heard him declare himself tired of life.\n\nFor three months afterward I fed current from a battery of two thousand pairs of plates through the great diamond, then labored a month at the grinding and polishing, the severest work of my life. At last the lens lay finished on the table. I sealed a drop of water under oil, flooded it with light, and pressed my eye to the minute bore. Chaos came first, then a pure white illumination without limit, for I had passed beyond infusoria and protozoa clean down to the original gaseous globule. There stretched avenues of foliated clouds and dim transparent forests painted with prismatic hues of unimaginable brilliance.\n\nAnd in that radiance moved a form, a female shape having the outlines of humanity but lifted beyond the loveliest daughter of Adam. Her motions were those of a naiad cleaving unruffled waters by mere effort of will. A living branch bent down to offer her shining fruit, and she ate it from her delicate hand. When a summons swept her out of sight behind the opaline trees I suffered an agony of jealousy, for I understood that somewhere in her luminous world she had a mate, and that I, a wretched giant peering through a hole in a stone, had fallen in love with an animalcule.\n\nWorse than that, every time my eye left the lens it fell upon a colorless bead of water on the slide. That tiny sphere was her whole universe and the wall of our separation. I wasted to a shadow before the instrument, and one morning found my sylph herself grown haggard, her golden hair lusterless, her fairy forests fading to gray. Then I remembered that I had not renewed the evaporating drop for days. The awful truth burst upon me. I had been gazing at its last remaining atom, and Animula was dying before my eyes. I watched the rainbow forests melt away and the final struggle end, and fainted across the wreck of my apparatus.\n\nThey say now that I am mad, and perhaps they are right. I am poor, I live on charity, and young men's clubs pay me to lecture on optics so they may laugh at Linley the mad microscopist. But who could talk sense whose brain is haunted, among the shapes of death, by the radiant memory of my lost Animula?",
