    [
        'slug' => 'nature-white-heron',
        'gutenberg_id' => 74980,
        'title' => 'A White Heron',
        'author' => 'Sarah Orne Jewett',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science/Nature'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/nature-white-heron.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A shy nine-year-old farm girl climbs a giant pine at dawn to learn a white heron\'s secret, then finds she cannot sell that secret for ten dollars.',
        'text' => "The woods were already filled with shadows one June evening, just before eight o'clock, though a bright sunset still glimmered faintly among the trunks of the trees. A little girl was driving home her cow, a plodding, provoking creature who loved to hide among the huckleberry bushes, where her bell would not ring if she stood perfectly still.\n\nSylvia had hunted for old Moolly until her patience was spent, and yet she did not really mind, for the child had all the time there was and very little use to make of it. Everybody said it was a good change for a little maid who had tried to grow for eight years in a crowded manufacturing town, but Sylvia herself felt as if she had never been alive at all before she came to her grandmother's farm. She had been a child afraid of folks. Now the woods were full of little birds and beasts going about their world or saying goodnight in sleepy twitters, and she walked part of the gray shadows and the moving leaves.\n\nSuddenly she heard a clear whistle not far away, not a bird's whistle, which would have had a friendliness about it, but a boy's whistle, determined and somewhat aggressive. A tall young man with a gun over his shoulder had lost his way, and he needed lodging for the night and a guide for his morning hunt.\n\nGrandmother Tilley welcomed him gladly, set a plate for the gentleman, and talked while the moon came up. The stranger was making a collection of birds, he explained, shooting and stuffing them with his own hands, dozens and dozens of them. There were two or three very rare ones he had hunted for five years. Only last Saturday he had caught a glimpse of a white heron three miles from here, a bird never found in this district at all, and he offered ten dollars to anybody who could show him its nest.\n\nSylvia's heart gave a wild beat. She knew that strange white bird. Once she had stolen softly near where it stood in bright green swamp grass across the woods, beyond the salt marshes, near the sea whose great voice she often heard on stormy nights but had never looked upon.\n\nAll the next day she kept the friendly lad company. He gave her a jack-knife, told her what the birds knew and where they lived, and never once made her afraid except when he brought down some unsuspecting singing creature from its bough. She could not understand why he killed the very things he seemed to love, and she did not lead him to her secret, only followed behind with her gray eyes dark with excitement.\n\nThat evening she thought of the great pine tree at the far edge of the woods, the last of its generation, whose stately head towered above the whole forest as a landmark for sea and shore. Whoever climbed to the top could see the ocean, everyone believed. If she climbed it at break of day, she could watch the heron fly and mark the hidden nest.\n\nThe whippoorwills sang upon the very step, and the household slept, but Sylvia's great design kept her broad awake. Before light she stole out and began the climb, first up a white oak growing alongside, then out along a swaying limb, then the dangerous step across into the pine. Sharp dry twigs caught and scratched her like angry talons, pitch made her thin fingers clumsy, and the great stem seemed to lengthen as she mounted, round and round, higher and higher, like a mainmast to the voyaging earth. The old tree stood still and held away the winds that June morning while the dawn grew bright in the east.\n\nAt the top, trembling and tired but wholly triumphant, she looked down on two hawks that seemed to fly low beneath her feet, on white sails far out at sea, on woodlands and villages reaching miles into the distance. Then a white spot rose from a dead hemlock like a single floating feather, swept past the landmark pine with outstretched slender neck, perched on a bough not far beyond hers, called back to his mate on the nest, and plumed his feathers for the new day. The solemn bird went away only when a company of shouting catbirds vexed him, and Sylvia knew his secret now.\n\nBut when she came down, pale and torn and smeared with pine pitch, and the grandmother and the guest stood questioning her in the doorway, Sylvia did not speak. His kind appealing eyes were looking straight into hers, and the ten dollars could make them rich. Yet the murmur of the pine's green branches was in her ears, she remembered how the heron came flying through the golden air and how they watched the sea and the morning together, and she could not tell the secret and give its life away.\n\nThe guest went away disappointed, and many a night afterward Sylvia heard the echo of his whistle haunting the pasture path as she came home with the loitering cow. Were the birds better friends than their hunter might have been? Who can tell? Whatever treasures were lost to her, woodlands and summer time, remember! Bring your gifts and graces and tell your secrets to this lonely country child.",
