    [
        'slug' => 'romance-last-leaf',
        'gutenberg_id' => 3707,
        'title' => 'The Last Leaf',
        'author' => 'O. Henry',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Romance'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'easy',
        'cover' => 'covers/romance-last-leaf.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'In a Greenwich Village studio a dying girl pins her life to an ivy vine, and a failed old painter answers her despair with his first masterpiece.',
        'text' => "In a little district west of Washington Square the streets run crazy and break into small strips called places, which is why the artists came prowling to quaint old Greenwich Village, hunting north windows, Dutch attics, and low rents.\n\nAt the top of a squatty three-story brick house, Sue and Johnsy had their studio. They had met at a table on Eighth Street and found their tastes in art and chicory salad so congenial that the joint studio resulted.\n\nThat was in May. In November a cold unseen stranger, whom the doctors called Pneumonia, stalked about the colony, touching one here and there with his icy fingers. Johnsy he smote, and she lay scarcely moving on her painted iron bedstead, looking through the small panes at the blank side of the next brick house.\n\nOne morning the doctor summoned Sue into the hallway. \"She has one chance in, let us say, ten,\" he said. \"And that chance is for her to want to live. Your little lady has made up her mind that she is not going to get well. Has she anything on her mind?\"\n\n\"She wanted to paint the Bay of Naples some day,\" said Sue.\n\nAfter the doctor left, Sue cried into a Japanese napkin, then swaggered into the sickroom whistling ragtime and set up her drawing board.\n\nSoon she heard a low sound repeated several times. Johnsy's eyes were open wide, fixed on the window, counting backward. Outside there was nothing to see but a bare dreary yard, the blank brick wall twenty feet away, and an old ivy vine, gnarled and decayed at its roots, climbing halfway up the bricks. The cold breath of autumn had stricken its leaves until the skeleton branches clung almost bare.\n\n\"Leaves,\" whispered Johnsy. \"On the ivy vine. When the last one falls, I must go too. There are only five left now.\"\n\n\"Oh, I never heard such nonsense,\" complained Sue with magnificent scorn. \"Try to take some broth, and let Sudie finish her drawing so she can sell it and buy port wine for her sick child.\"\n\nBut Johnsy kept her eyes on the window. \"I want to see the last one fall before it gets dark. Then I'll go too. I'm tired of thinking. I want to turn loose my hold on everything and go sailing down, down, just like one of those poor tired leaves.\"\n\n\"I must call Behrman up to be my model,\" said Sue, and went downstairs.\n\nOld Behrman was a painter who lived on the ground floor beneath them. He was past sixty, with a curling beard like a prophet's, and he was a failure in art. For forty years he had wielded the brush without ever getting near his masterpiece, which he was always about to begin. He earned a little by modeling for young artists who could not pay professionals, drank gin to excess, scoffed terribly at softness in anyone, and regarded himself as a fierce mastiff-in-waiting to protect the two girls above.\n\nSue found him in his dimly lit den, told him of Johnsy's fancy, and how she feared that Johnsy, light and fragile as a leaf herself, would float away when her hold on the world grew weaker. The old man shouted his contempt for such idiocy, and then insisted on coming upstairs at once, declaring that some day he would paint a masterpiece and they should all go away.\n\nA persistent cold rain mixed with snow fell that night, and the two women peered fearfully at the vine from the other room without speaking.\n\nWhen Sue woke the next morning, Johnsy commanded in a whisper that the shade be raised. After the beating rain and fierce gusts of wind that had endured through the livelong night, one ivy leaf yet stood out against the brick wall. It was the last on the vine, still dark green near its stem, its edges tinted with yellow, and it hung bravely from a branch some twenty feet above the ground.\n\n\"I thought it would surely fall during the night,\" said Johnsy. \"I heard the wind. But it has not fallen, and I have been a bad girl, Sudie. Something has made that last leaf stay there to show me how wicked it is to want to die. Bring me a little broth now, and a hand mirror, and pack some pillows about me.\"\n\nAn hour later she said, \"Sudie, some day I hope to paint the Bay of Naples.\"\n\nThat afternoon the doctor took Sue's thin shaking hand in the hallway. \"Even chances,\" he said. \"With good nursing you'll win.\" Downstairs waited another case, an old weak man named Behrman, an artist, stricken with pneumonia, and there was no hope for him.\n\nThe next day the doctor pronounced Johnsy out of danger, and Sue came to sit on the bed beside her.\n\n\"I have something to tell you, white mouse,\" she said. \"Mr. Behrman died of pneumonia today in the hospital. He was ill only two days. The janitor found him helpless with pain, his shoes and clothing wet through and icy cold, and nobody could imagine where he had been on such a dreadful night. Then they found a lantern still lighted, a ladder dragged from its place, scattered brushes, and a palette with green and yellow colors mixed on it. Look out the window, dear, at the last ivy leaf on the wall. Didn't you wonder why it never fluttered or moved when the wind blew? Ah, darling, it is Behrman's masterpiece. He painted it there the night the last real leaf fell.\"",
