    [
        'slug' => 'nature-pacing-mustang',
        'gutenberg_id' => 3031,
        'title' => 'The Pacing Mustang',
        'author' => 'Ernest Thompson Seton',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science/Nature'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'hard',
        'cover' => 'covers/nature-pacing-mustang.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A matchless black mustang outruns ropes, pitfalls, and relay chases across the New Mexico plains until an old cook\'s love-trap brings him down, and freedom costs his life.',
        'text' => "At the roundup camp on the Perico, Wild Jo Calone told of a mustang bunch that watered at Antelope Springs, and of a little dark colt that led the band a mile or two without ever breaking his pace. A year later the colt was a glossy black yearling, and more than one cowboy had seen the oddity with his own eyes. The mustang was a born pacer.\n\nIn that country an unbroken mustang was worth five dollars and commonly got shot as vermin, but Jo had heard it said that a black bronk was hard as nails and full of the old Harry, and he quietly made the Black Mustang his mascot. He was only a cow-puncher on twenty-five a month, yet he dreamed of making a strike someday, and the strike he dreamed of was that horse.\n\nThe Pacer's fame grew. Nine thoroughbred mares strayed from the L cross F range, and when the manager rode after them he found them guarded by the coal-black stallion, who rounded up his golden harem like an expert and swept it away beyond all pursuit. His following rose to twenty mares, and the ranchmen realized they had gained on their range a mustang doing them more harm than every other loss together.\n\nI was new in the country that winter, and my guide Jack Burns dropped flat on his horse when the famous band appeared below a ridge. There stood the Great Black Mustang, head and tail erect, nostrils wide, an image of horse perfection, and I found the notion of turning that magnificent creature into a mass of carrion simply horrible. Burns swore at my slowness and snatched for the gun, which went off in the struggle. The herd rumbled away in a cloud of dust, the black leader watching all and driving all, and never once did he break his pace.\n\nWhen old Montgomery offered a thousand dollars for the Stallion safe in a box car, Wild Jo mounted the classic walk-down. For five days he and his pard followed the herd in relays, coaxing it along a great circle whose chord the mess wagon cut. The mares wore out, and a final water gorge at the springs ruined what wind they had left, so the band fell into Jo's power. But the prize of the hunt seemed made of iron. Jo had watched him day after day at speed and never once seen him gallop, and the horseman's adoration grew until he would sooner have shot his best saddle horse. On a locoed mare of Eastern blood he tried one last burst, and the Black One led away across mesa and sand with his start growing longer by the inch, until the crazed brute stepped in a badger hole and fell and had to be put out of pain. Jo drove the captured mares home and claimed his reward, but he wanted the Stallion more than ever.\n\nOthers took up the hunt. Horseshoe Billy told how he had come upon the Pacer lying plumb flat on the dry mud, dead as a mackerel, until an ear twitched off a fly, and how he weighed his shaky old rope and seven-hundred-pound pony against twelve hundred pounds of stallion and decided to crack his saddle horn instead, whereupon the sleeper leaped six feet in the air and departed for California without once breaking stride. Then Turkeytrack and Billy dug a pit in the drinking trail at the springs and covered it cunningly with poles and brush. Yet what sleepless angel watches over the wild things? The Stallion came down the other path, and when at last they fired shots behind him and raced him straight at the trap, he cleared all fifteen feet of treacherous ground in one mighty bound, and never again visited the springs by either beaten path.\n\nSo Jo tried the grand relay chase over the sixty-mile triangle of the Pacer's range, with grain-fed horses posted at the crossings. An hour, another hour, and another hour went to the measured beat of those tireless hoofs ahead. Fresh riders took up the race at Arriba Canon and Alamosa ford, men fired dust at the turns, one spent horse drank himself to death at the river, and still not a single inch was gained. It ended at morning with the tale briefly told. Eight horses dead, five men worn out, the matchless Pacer safe and free.\n\nOld Turkeytrack alone knew what force persecution could not touch. All winter the Stallion had lived lonely since the loss of his harem, so the old cook picketed a gentle brown mare near the springs and hid himself in a pit beside a roped and buried noose. About noon her long shrill whinny of love was answered from the west, and there against the sky stood the famous Mustang, coming down at his swinging gait. The angel of the wild things whispered to him, and he circled and tried the wind, but the mare called again and set his heart aglow. He touched noses with her, pranced about his conquest, and for one moment his hind feet stood within the evil circle of the rope. The noose flew tight, the second lasso bound his forefeet, and down went the raging Pacer, hog-tied and hopeless, sobbing great convulsive sobs while tears ran down his cheeks.\n\nUnder Western law a wild horse belonged to the first man who marked him with a brand, and the nearest iron was twenty miles away. So Tom pried a shoe from the mare's hoof, heated it over a buffalo-chip fire, and seared his turkeytrack upon the helpless shoulder. Then began the drive north toward the canyon, every yard a fight, the glossy sides thick with dark foam and the foam stained with blood.\n\nFrom the head of the draw the first corral was in sight, and the man rejoiced. But the Mustang gathered his remaining strength for one more dash. Up the grassy slope he went, defying the slashing rope and the gun, and above the sheerest cliff he sprang away into the vacant air, two hundred downward feet to fall, and land upon the rocks below, a lifeless wreck, but free.",
