        'synopsis' => 'A young house mongoose fights a war against two king cobras to defend the English family that saved him from a flood.',
        'text' => "This is the story of the great war that Rikki-tikki-tavi fought single-handed through the bathrooms of the big bungalow in Segowlee cantonment.\n\nHe was a mongoose, rather like a little cat in his fur and his tail, but quite like a weasel in his head and habits. His eyes and the tip of his restless nose were pink, he could fluff his tail into a bottle brush, and his war cry through the long grass sounded like Rikk-tikk-tikki-tikki-tchk.\n\nOne day a high summer flood washed him out of his burrow and carried him, kicking and clucking, down a roadside ditch, until a small boy found him lying senseless on a garden path and cried that here was a dead mongoose and they must have a funeral. His mother said perhaps he was not really dead, so they wrapped him in cotton wool and warmed him, and he opened his eyes and sneezed.\n\nIt is the hardest thing in the world to frighten a mongoose, because he is eaten up from nose to tail with curiosity. The motto of the whole family is run and find out. Rikki-tikki proved himself a true mongoose at once, inspecting the cotton wool, patrolling the table, and jumping onto the boy Teddy's shoulder to tickle under his chin. Before nightfall he had nearly drowned in a bathtub, put his nose into the inkwell, and singed it on a cigar, and when Teddy went to sleep the little animal lay awake on the pillow attending to every noise. Teddy's father said his son was safer with that little beast than with a bloodhound.\n\nThe next morning Rikki went out to inspect the garden, half wild bushes and roses and thickets of high grass, a splendid hunting ground, and there he heard sorrowful voices. Darzee the tailorbird and his wife were weeping over their cleverly stitched nest, for one of their babies had fallen out yesterday, and Nag ate him.\n\nWho is Nag, asked Rikki, and the grass at the foot of the bush answered with a low cold hiss. Inch by inch rose the head and spread hood of Nag, the big black cobra, five feet long from tongue to tail, balancing exactly as a dandelion tuft balances on the wind. The great God Brahm put his mark upon our people when the first cobra spread his hood to shade the sleeping god, said Nag. Look, and be afraid. Rikki was afraid for one minute, but a mongoose cannot stay frightened, and though he had never met a live cobra, his mother had fed him on dead ones. He knew that all a grown mongoose's business in life was to fight and eat snakes, and Nag knew it too, and at the bottom of his cold heart he was afraid.\n\nWhile they talked, Darzee sang a warning. Look behind you! Rikki jumped straight up, and just beneath him whizzed the striking head of Nagaina, the wicked wife, who had crept close during the conversation. He came down almost across her back and bit, but not long enough, and sprang clear of the lashing tail, leaving her torn and angry. When a snake misses its stroke it says nothing and gives no sign, so Rikki sat back like a little kangaroo and thought things over.\n\nHis first real victory came soon after, over Karait, the dusty brown snakeling whose bite is as deadly as a cobra's precisely because he is so small that nobody fears him. Eyes red with anger, rocking in the swaying dance his family had taught him, Rikki waited until the little serpent lunged too far, then leaped upon his back and bit as high up the neck as he could hold. That bite paralyzed Karait. But Rikki remembered that a full meal makes a slow mongoose, and left the tail end uneaten to keep himself quick.\n\nThat night he learned how much danger remained. Chuchundra the muskrat, a broken-hearted little beast who creeps forever around the walls and never dares cross the middle of a room, sobbed out his warnings until Rikki caught the faintest scratch-scratch in the world, like a wasp walking on a windowpane. It was the dry rasp of snake scales on brickwork, and it led to the bathroom sluice. There in the moonlight he heard the cobras plotting. Kill the big man first, whispered Nagaina, then the wife, then the child if you can, and when the bungalow stands empty we shall be king and queen of the garden again, for our eggs in the melon bed may hatch any day.\n\nNag slid through the sluice and coiled himself around the bottom of the great water jar to wait for morning, when the man would come unarmed to bathe. Rikki attacked in the dark. The head, he told himself, the head above the hood, and once there I must never let go. His teeth met, he braced his back against the jar, and then he was battered about the bathroom like a rat shaken by a dog, up and down, in great circles, holding on while tin dishes crashed and sure that he would be banged to pieces. Then something thundered behind him and hot wind and red fire filled the room, for the big man had wakened and fired both barrels of a shotgun just behind the hood. Rikki held on with shut eyes until it did not matter anymore.\n\nIn the morning he faced Nagaina alone, who would be worse than five Nags. Darzee sang triumph while his wiser wife flew off to play broken-winged and lure the widow away. Rikki raced for the melon bed and found twenty-five cobra eggs hidden in the warm litter, and he crushed every one but three before the screaming warning came that Nagaina stood swaying beside Teddy's chair on the veranda, within striking distance of the child's bare leg, promising that if they moved she would strike and if they did not move she would strike.\n\nLook at your friends, she hissed at Rikki. They are still and white. Come one step nearer and I strike.\n\nThen Rikki lifted the last egg between his paws and named its price, and the mother in her forgot everything else and spun round to bargain. In that instant Teddy's father shot out his hand and dragged the boy safe across the tea table, and Rikki began his mocking war cry, boasting how he had held Nag by the hood until the guns spoke. Come then, Nagaina, he shouted. You will not be a widow long.\n\nShe struck again and again, each blow whacking the matting, until at last she snatched the egg and flew like an arrow down the path toward the rat hole where she and Nag used to live. Rikki hung on her tail as she plunged underground, and very few mongooses care to follow a cobra down its own dark hole. What happened below the earth only the red ants between the grass stems ever knew, for they trooped down one after another to see, and found the matter finished.\n\nDarzee was singing a mournful death song for Rikki when the grass quivered and the hero dragged himself out leg by leg, covered with dirt, licking his whiskers. It is all over, he sneezed. The widow will never come out again.\n\nThe Coppersmith bird beat his little dinner gong all evening with the news, and the frogs croaked joyfully, for the cobras had eaten frogs as well as fledglings. That night Rikki ate until he could eat no more and fell asleep on Teddy's shoulder, and when the mother tiptoed in to look, he woke with a jump. Oh, it's you, he said. All the cobras are dead. And if they weren't, I'm here. He kept that garden as a mongoose should keep it, with tooth and jump and spring and bite, till never a cobra dared show its head inside the walls again.",
